<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">International Association of the Congo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1879–1885</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The International Association of the Congo was an organisation controlled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-morton-stanley">Henry Morton Stanley</a> built stations along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a> and collected treaties from local rulers. Presented as a philanthropic and scientific body, it was recognised as a sovereign power by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1884 and by the powers at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a>, and became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> in 1885.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/international-association-of-the-congo.jpg" alt="International Association of the Congo" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See file history below for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Congo_Free_State.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
